\subsection{Mixed-Methods Clinical Data Analysis using
MLLMs}\label{mixed-methods-clinical-data-analysis-using-mllms}

\begin{itemize}
\item
  \textbf{Overview:}

  \begin{itemize}
  \item
    This project evaluated the feasibility of utilizing locally hosted
    Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs) to computationally
    integrate and jointly analyze qualitative and quantitative clinical
    trial data.
  \item
    Using a dataset from the LQD trial for treatment-resistant
    depression, three mixed-method analytical tasks---Convergence,
    Explanatory, and Statistical---were applied to the data using an
    open-source MLLM.
  \item
    Due to the absence of a definitive human ground truth, an automated
    ``LLM-as-a-Judge'' evaluation strategy was implemented to
    systematically assess the model's analytical performance.
  \end{itemize}
\item
  \textbf{Key Findings:}

  \begin{itemize}
  \item
    The MLLM demonstrated strong overall performance across the
    mixed-method tasks, achieving high factual consistency and
    effectively generating functional Python code for quantitative data
    analysis.
  \item
    The model successfully analyzed complex logical relationships,
    effectively bridging qualitative patient narratives with
    quantitative data trajectories.
  \item
    While the model showed robust reasoning abilities, a primary
    limitation observed was its inherent lack of deep, domain-specific
    clinical knowledge, which required targeted prompting and context to
    overcome.
  \end{itemize}
\item
  \textbf{My Contribution:}

  \begin{itemize}
  \item
    I designed the three mixed-method research tasks and developed the
    multi-stage ``chain-of-thought'' prompting pipelines used to guide
    the model's analysis.
  \item
    I created the specific scoring rubrics utilized by the independent
    ``LLM-as-a-Judge'' to evaluate the model's factual consistency,
    coding robustness, and integrative synthesis.
  \item
    I managed the technical deployment of the models on King's College
    London's High Performance Cluster (CREATE), utilizing technical
    strategies like model quantization in 4-bit precision and data
    batching to successfully overcome Out-Of-Memory (OOM) errors.
  \end{itemize}
\end{itemize}

